{
  "teamId": 138,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 St. Louis Cardinals season was one of the most painful in recent franchise memory — a steady, grinding collapse that turned a perennial playoff contender into a 71-91 also-ran. The wounds were visible from the start. April saw the Cardinals go 10-18, a brutal hole they never escaped, even as flashes of individual brilliance — most notably from young Nolan Gorman — offered brief sparks of hope. The bullpen, anchored by a struggling Giovanny Gallegos and an inconsistent Ryan Helsley, proved to be a recurring source of agony, surrendering leads at the worst possible moments.\n\nMay offered a momentary exhale, the Cardinals posting their only winning month at 15-13. Brendan Donovan's clutch three-run homer answered a Jake Rogers grand slam in a microcosm of the season's emotional whiplash. But June brought the walls crashing back in at 8-15, with Gallegos continuing to hemorrhage runs and the roster showing the cracks of an aging core without sufficient depth. Tommy Edman's grand slam provided one glorious afternoon, only for Gallegos to surrender another home run in the same game.\n\nJuly was a study in futility disguised as mediocrity — a 14-14 record that felt like treading water while drowning. Jordan Walker's electric two-run homer on July 5th gave the crowd something to believe in, only for Jordan Hicks to surrender the lead in the very same game. The back half of the summer — August and September — confirmed what the standings already knew: this team was not built to compete. The lone bright light was Tommy Edman's walk-off homer on August 30th, a moment of genuine electricity in an otherwise forgettable stretch.\n\nIn the end, the 2023 Cardinals were a team undone by a leaky bullpen, a historically bad April, and the slow fade of a roster that had carried a dynasty as far as it could go. With 829 runs allowed against 719 scored, the math was never in their favor. The season finished 71-91, out of the playoffs for the first time in years, leaving an organization facing hard questions about what comes next as a new generation — Masyn Winn, Jordan Walker — began to quietly announce itself amid the wreckage.",
